---
const facts = [
  { k: 'currently', v: 'Front-end engineer at SENAI CIMATEC, Salvador' },
  { k: 'recognition', v: 'Two-time global nominee, NASA Space Apps (2024, 2025)' },
  { k: 'education', v: 'B.Sc. Computer Science — 10/10 thesis on Solidity smart contracts' },
];
---

<section id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title" class="border-b border-line">
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[1.6fr_1fr]">
    <div>
      <div class="mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 border-b border-line pb-4">
        <h2 id="about-title" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-ink/90">
        <p>
          I started on the wrong side of the screen — electrotechnics, industrial
          automation, PLCs that fail loudly when you get them wrong. Programming
          was the tool that made those systems behave. Web development won me over
          by keeping the same discipline and dropping most of the waiting: the
          feedback loop went from days to seconds, and the audience went from one
          factory to everyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Six years later, most of that time went into front-end at scale: component
          libraries shared across modern and legacy stacks at SENAI CIMATEC, 170+
          institutional sites and e-learning platforms kept fast and accessible,
          interfaces that had to survive real users on real networks. The
          engineering productivity gains there — tenfold, by our own measurement —
          came from systems, not heroics: reusable components, standard workflows,
          code review with teeth.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pull back toward full-stack came from the seams: I kept integrating
          the APIs and wanted to own the other side of the contract. This site is
          where that claim gets tested in the open — the form below is a typed
          server action, the counters are in Redis, and the whole thing deploys as
          both a serverless app and a static mirror from one codebase.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside aria-label="Facts">
      <dl class="divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
        {
          facts.map((fact) => (
            <div class="grid gap-1 py-5">
              <dt class="text-xs text-dim">{fact.k}</dt>
              <dd class="text-sm text-ink">{fact.v}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>
